@berlin2123 to adjust the table you need to log into the postgres DB where it is stored then run the query I provided (with the proper size in place of where I put '768' depending on your specific use case)

If there is already data in the table you will get an error message, in that case you need to run the query `truncate table embeddings` which will delete all data in the table and allow you to modify the columns. 

If you change to another model with different vector size you will need to repeat these steps to match the column width again


